Potential flash opacity: it's child gun, okay.

Now post.js. Need render target, shader. Implement createPost(renderer). Use HalfFloatType? WebGL2; for SwiftShader maybe. Use `THREE.HalfFloatType`. In shader, we need tonemap. Renderer outputColorSpace? We set `renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.LinearSRGBColorSpace`? For rendering to RT, output color space linear regardless? Three applies encoding at material shader based on outputColorSpace of renderer when rendering to canvas only? Actually WebGLRenderer sets outputColorSpace and in `WebGLProgram` uses `outputColorSpace` to add colorspace_fragment for all renders, including render targets? It checks renderTarget's texture colorSpace. For RT default NoColorSpace -> linear. Good. We'll set renderer.toneMapping = NoToneMapping.

Post shader:
```
uniform sampler2D tDiffuse; uniform vec2 uResolution; uniform float uTime; ...
vec3 aces(vec3 x)...
float hash...
void main() {
 vec2 uv = vUv;
 vec2 p = uv-0.5;
 float r2=dot(p,p);
 // barrel distortion
 vec2 duv = 0.5 + p*(1.0 + 0.075*r2 + 0.03*r2*r2);
 // slight aspect correction? radial works.
 // chromatic
 vec2 ca = p * (0.0012 + r2*0.0025);
 float r = texture2D(tDiffuse, duv + ca).r;
 float g = texture2D(tDiffuse, duv).g;
 float b = texture2D(tDiffuse, duv - ca).b;
 vec3 col=...
 // glow: sample 4 taps around with luminance
 vec3 bloom=...
 col += bloom*0.15;
 // color grade
 float luma=...
 col = mix(vec3(luma), col, 1.08);
 col = pow(col, vec3(0.94,1.0,1.06)); // teal shadows? actually gamma
 col.rgb = col.rgb*vec3(1.03,1.0,0.96);
 // lift shadows
 col += vec3(0.005,0.008,0.012)*(1-luma);
 // vignette
 float vig = smoothstep(0.9,0.25, length(p)*1.25); // careful
 col *= mix(0.55,1.0,vig)
 // grain
 float grain = hash(uv*uResolution + uTime*60.0)-0.5;
 col += grain * (0.045 + 0.02*(1-luma));
 // scanlines
 col *= 1.0 - 0.018*sin(uv.y*uResolution.y*1.4);
 // damage
 col = mix(col, vec3(0.5,0.02,0.01), uDamage * smoothstep(.2,.75,length(p))*0.7);
 // low health pulse edges
 // slight flicker
 col *= 0.985 + 0.015*sin(uTime*11.0)
 // tonemap
 col = aces(col*1.05);
 // gamma
 col = pow(col, vec3(1.0/2.2));
 // border black? no
 gl_FragColor=vec4(col,1.0);
}
```
Need be careful shader's `vUv` from plane vertex with `position` at -1..1? Use:
```
vertexShader: varying vec2 vUv; void main(){ vUv=uv; gl_Position=vec4(position.xy,0.,1.);}
```
Plane geometry 2x2.

Bloom taps. Could add radial blur for speed? no.

Lens dirt: add two soft smudges based on distance to points:
```
float smudge=0; for points... 
col *= 1.0 - smudge*0.04
```
Maybe static. Add low health red edges.

Also add `uFlash` for damage.

Need resize sets RT size. Use `renderer.getDrawingBufferSize`.

We need render scene and weapon scene to same RT. In main:
```
renderer.setRenderTarget(rt); renderer.clear(); renderer.render(scene,camera); renderer.clearDepth(); renderer.render(vm.scene, vm.camera);
renderer.setRenderTarget(null); renderer.render(postScene, postCamera);
```
With `renderer.autoClear=false`? Set to false, clear manually. Need clear color. `renderer.setClearColor(0x05070a,1)`.
But post scene background? set transparent? post full-screen. Fine.

Need ensure `renderer.outputColorSpace` default sRGB, but post shader manually gamma; the output material shader may add another sRGB conversion if renderer outputColorSpace = SRGB. In Three r160, output encoding is added if `renderTarget === null` and outputColorSpace = SRGB, via `colorspace_fragment`. For ShaderMaterial with custom fragment, Three injects `#include <colorspace_fragment>` only if material uses it? ShaderMaterial compiled with prefix? Actually custom ShaderMaterial doesn't automatically add colorspace fragment unless the shader includes it. So no double conversion. Good. But built-in materials rendered to RT with NoColorSpace will be linear. Great.

